  {
    "sectionId": "auto-repair:section:brake-system-inspection-and-measurement",
    "title": "Fundamentals of Brake System Inspection",
    "body": "The brake system is arguably the most critical safety system on any vehicle. Technicians must be proficient in measuring pad and shoe thickness using precision tools like digital calipers or graduated brake gauges. A visual inspection is insufficient; you must measure the friction material accurately to determine if it is within the manufacturer's wear limits. Rotors and drums must be inspected for scoring, heat checking, and warping. When checking the hydraulic system, focus on flexible brake hoses for signs of dry rot, cracking, or bulging under pressure, and inspect steel lines for corrosion. Furthermore, check the master cylinder reservoir level, as a significant drop often indicates either a leak or thinning brake pads that have caused the calipers to travel further. Always follow proper shop safety protocols when dealing with brake dust, ensuring appropriate filtration and handling methods.",
    "definition": "The brake system uses hydraulic pressure to actuate calipers or wheel cylinders, forcing friction material against spinning rotors or drums to convert kinetic energy into heat energy.",
    "whyItMatters": "Accurate brake inspection prevents catastrophic failure, ensures compliance with safety standards, and extends the life of expensive components like calipers and ABS modules.",
    "summary": "Brake maintenance relies on precise measurement of friction material and thorough inspection of the hydraulic lines and friction surfaces to ensure optimal stopping performance."
  }
